   138	normalization); equivalently `disc(I)=-27 disc(H_I)`.  This records the same
   139	invariant after a twisted `GL_2(R)` action.  It does not decide whether the
   140	cubic represents a unit: example (2.1) has irreducible reduced branch but no
   141	unit value.
   142	
   143	There is also an exact reason that selecting an arbitrary element cannot
   144	bypass (1.3).  For `theta=sz+tw`,
   145	
   146	```text
   147	disc(charpoly(theta))=I(s,t)^2 disc(B/R).              (3.2)
   148	```
   149	
   150	After translating `theta`, the left side has the form `4A^3+27B^2`, but in
   151	the nonmonogenic case it is `F` times the nonunit index square `I(s,t)^2`.
   152	The square creates extra apparent discriminant components and changes both
   153	the Newton polygon at infinity and the Bezout count used in charged r2
   154	sections 4 and 6.  Removing it without proof would simply assume the desired
   155	unit value.
   156	
   157	Nor can one run those sections on the five-term four-coefficient
   158	discriminant directly.  The four coefficients have no degree bounds after a
   159	trivialization of `E`, and
   160	
   161	```text
   162	B_0^2C_0^2-3A_0^2D_0^2+4A_0^3C_0+4B_0D_0^3
   163	 -6A_0B_0C_0D_0=kappa F                              (3.3)
   164	```
   165	
   166	allows cancellations among all five terms.  Local depression over completed
   167	local rings is available and is already reflected in the smooth-branch and
   168	node models of charged r2 section 1, but those local basis changes need not
   169	patch to `GL_2(R)`.  At a node they reproduce the allowed
   170	`Rhat direct-sum Rhat[t]/(t^2-uv)` model rather than a contradiction.  At
   171	infinity, without coefficient pole bounds, (3.3) merely pulls back the
   172	discriminant hypersurface to the prescribed `(2,15)` germ; the four-case
   173	bounded-degree exhaustion in r2 section 4 no longer applies.
   174	
   175	Thus a direct Miranda-jet proof would be a genuinely new lemma, not a
   176	rephrasing of the completed depressed calculation.  No such exhaustion is
   177	proved here.  The safe residual is the twisted `GL_2(R)` orbit of (1.1) with
   178	branch `kappa F` and no unit value, subject to the connected, normal and
   179	specified local-monodromy conditions.
   180	
   181	## 4. What Davenport--Mason actually implies
   182	
   183	Assume now, and only in this section onward, that a global power basis has
   184	been supplied.  Rescale by nonzero complex constants and write its exact
   185	branch identity as
   186	
   187	```text
   188	A^3-B^2=F,       deg F=6,                              (4.1)
   189	```
   190	
   191	where `F` is the irreducible row equation.  If the leading terms do not
   192	cancel, (4.1) immediately gives `deg A<=2`, `deg B<=3`.  If either bound
   193	fails, equality of the two top degrees is necessary:
   194	
   195	```text
   196	deg A=2k,  deg B=3k,  k>=2.                            (4.2)
   197	```
   198	
   199	For coprime one-variable polynomials `P,Q,H` with `P^3-Q^2=H`, the
   200	Mason--Stothers theorem gives
   201	
   202	```text
   203	6k <= deg rad(PQH)-1 <= 2k+3k+deg H-1.                (4.3)
   204	```
   205	
   206	Equivalently Davenport's inequality is
   207	`deg(P^3-Q^2)>=k+1`.  With `deg H=6`, (4.3) yields only `k<=5`.
   208	Therefore the exact numerical alternatives before using row geometry are
   209	
   210	```text
   211	(deg A,deg B)=(4,6),(6,9),(8,12),(10,15).             (4.4)
   212	```
   213	
   214	This corrects the dichotomy in the charge.  Only `(10,15)` is a
   215	Davenport-extremal (DS order-five) case, since only there `deg H=k+1`.
   216	The first three pairs are genuine nonextremal cancellation bands and are
   217	not classified away by the Stothers--Zannier equality families.  Shioda's
   218	displayed order-five identity proves sharpness: its degree-six remainder is
   219	squarefree, although its zero set is a union of parallel lines and hence is
   220	not the row.
   221	
   222	For completeness, (4.3) is the direct polynomial-abc calculation applied
   223	to `P^3+(-Q^2)+(-H)=0`: pairwise coprimality makes the radical degree at
   224	most `2k+3k+6`, while the largest summand has degree `6k`.  Thus no
   225	classification assertion is hidden in the inequality.  Equality at `k=5`
   226	forces all three radicals to have their maximal degrees and gives the
   227	three-critical-value/Belyi description used by Stothers and Zannier, but a
   228	normalization of such a one-variable triple is allowed to vary with the
   229	chosen line.  Promoting it to a fixed two-variable family would be invalid.
   230	
   231	## 5. Generic lines and the row-specific cancellation lemma
   232	
   233	The specialization used in section 4 is uniform on a nonempty Zariski-open
   234	set of affine lines.  The coefficient of the top power of the line parameter
   235	is the leading homogeneous form evaluated at its direction, so avoiding the
   236	three corresponding zero sets preserves the degrees of `A,B,F`.  A further
   237	open condition makes the line transverse to the reduced sextic and avoids
   238	its three nodes, hence `F|_ell` has six simple roots.
   239	
   240	There is no hidden gcd failure.  If `A(p)=B(p)=0`, then `F(p)=0` and `p` is
   241	singular.  The quadratic tangent cone of `A^3-B^2` is the square of the
   242	linear part of `B` when that part is nonzero; if it vanishes, the
   243	multiplicity is at least three.  Such a point is never an ordinary node.
   244	The row has no other affine singularities, so `V(A,B)` is empty.  Therefore
   245	the restrictions, and each of them with `F|_ell`, are pairwise coprime.
   246	This proves all hypotheses of (4.3) on one common open set and makes the
   247	degree-semicontinuity step airtight.
   248	
   249	The row geometry now eliminates all four bands in (4.4).  Let
   250	`C=V(F)`.  The images of `A,B` in `O(C)` have no zero, hence are units.  On
   251	`C`, `B^2=A^3`, so
   252	
   253	```text
   254	t=B/A,       t^2=A,       t^3=B.                      (5.1)
   255	```
   256	
   257	The projective normalization of `C` has exactly one point over infinity.
   258	The divisor of the pulled-back unit `t` is supported at that one point and
   259	has degree zero, so it is zero; a nowhere-zero regular function on the
   260	projective normalization is constant.  Thus `t=c in C^*`, and for some
   261	`P,Q in R`,
   262	
   263	```text
   264	A=c^2+FP,          B=c^3+FQ.                          (5.2)
   265	```
   266	
   267	In particular `F` divides the Jacobian `J(A,B)`.
   268	
   269	Suppose (4.2) holds.  Introduce the polynomial one-form
   270	
   271	```text
   272	omega=2A dB-3B dA.                                   (5.3)
   273	```
   274	
   275	Differentiating (4.1) and using `B^2=A^3-F` gives two exact identities:
   276	
   277	```text
   278	B omega=3F dA-A dF,        d omega=5 dA wedge dB.     (5.4)
   279	```
   280	
   281	The coefficients on the right of the first identity have degree at most
   282	`2k+5`.  Hence, if `omega` is nonzero, its coefficients have degree at most
   283	`5-k`.  The second identity then gives
   284	`deg J(A,B)<=4-k<6`; if `omega=0`, it gives `J(A,B)=0` directly.  Since the
   285	irreducible degree-six polynomial `F` divides this Jacobian, in every case
   286	
   287	```text
   288	J(A,B)=0.                                              (5.5)
   289	```
   290	
   291	Use the polynomial Luroth lemma: two algebraically dependent polynomials in
   292	`C[x,y]` lie in `C[r]` for a polynomial `r`.  Here is the needed short
   293	justification.  Normalize `C[A,B]` in its fraction field.  A general line
   294	in `P^2` maps nontrivially to the smooth projective model of that field, so
   295	Riemann--Hurwitz makes the model `P^1`.  The normalization is therefore the
   296	coordinate ring of `P^1-S`.  It lies in the integrally closed ring
   297	`C[x,y]`, so all its units are constants.  If `S` contained two points,
   298	`P^1-S` would have a nonconstant unit with divisor supported on those two
   299	points.  Hence `S` is one point and the normalization is `C[r]`, with
   300	`r in C[x,y]`.  Thus
   301	`A=U(r), B=V(r)` and `F=W(r)=U(r)^3-V(r)^2`.  Because `C` is algebraically
   302	closed and `F` is irreducible, `W` must be linear; otherwise its linear
   303	factors give a nontrivial factorization of `W(r)`.  Hence `r` is affine in
   304	`F` and `A,B` belong to `C[F]`.  Every positive degree among them is
   305	therefore divisible by six.  None of the four pairs in (4.4) has both
   306	degrees divisible by six.  This contradiction proves the promised exact
   307	lemma:
   308	
   309	```text
   310	If the depressed cubic has the row's irreducible one-place sextic branch
   311	and only ordinary affine nodes, then deg A<=2 and deg B<=3.              (5.6)
   312	```
   313	
   314	In particular the set of Davenport-extremal families compatible with the
   315	row is empty.  The argument treats the two-variable pair globally; it does
   316	not identify line-dependent normalizations.
   317	
   318	## 6. Assembly: the strongest theorem proved
   319	
   320	**Theorem (monogenic-resolvent kill).**  Assume the provisional charged row
   321	data: `D` is an irreducible reduced affine sextic, its affine singularities
   322	are exactly three ordinary nodes, and its projective normalization has one
   323	point over infinity with the charged `Delta=(6,4,3)` / `(2,15)` germ
   324	(`mult=2`, intersection six).  Suppose there is a surjection
   325	`phi:pi_1(A^2-D)->S_4` taking meridians to transpositions and satisfying the
   326	charged node relations.  Let `B` be the cubic algebra obtained from the
   327	quotient `S_4->S_3`.  If `B` has a global power basis over `C[x,y]`, then no
   328	such `phi` exists.
   329	
   330	The charged r2 report proves, without monogenicity, that this quotient gives
   331	a connected normal finite-flat triple cover and that
   332	`disc(B)=kappa F` with multiplicity one (lines 35--84).  Under the one extra
   333	hypothesis, section 1 gives (1.4), and the cancellation lemma (5.6) now
   334	proves rather than assumes
   335	
   336	```text
   337	deg a<=2,       deg b<=3.                              (6.1)
   338	```
   339	
   340	The completed charged contradiction can therefore be consumed at its exact
   341	scope.  Its leading-form classification (r2 lines 167--222) and infinity
   342	calculation (lines 224--270) show that the possible orders are
   343	`2,3,4,6`, whereas the row's one `(2,15)` place requires order `5`.
   344	Independently, its affine-node/Bezout argument (lines 272--365) forces the
   345	quadratic coefficient to be constant and the sextic to factor.  Either is a
   346	contradiction.
   347	
   348	There is no longer a cancellation residual: sections 4--5 include the three
   349	nonextremal bands omitted by the proposed dichotomy and all order-five
   350	Stothers--Zannier extremals.  The sole remaining logical class is
   351	
   352	```text
   353	OPEN[PI1S4-(6,4)-TRIPLE-COVER-GLOBAL-MONOGENICITY-ROW]:
   354	the actual resolvent index form has no value in C^*.                   (6.2)
   355	```
   356	
   357	Equivalently, any surviving `S_4` representation is now proved to have a
   358	nonmonogenic resolvent.  Example (2.1) shows that this conclusion is not
   359	formally impossible, but it does not realize the row.
   360	
   361	This is not an unconditional uniform kill of `(6,4)`.  For `(8,4)`, the
   362	coordinator records only a provisional target-automorphism equivalence to
   363	the `(6,4)` survivor.  A genuine target automorphism preserves both the
   364	complement and monogenicity, so the theorem transfers at that provisional
   365	scope.  The hostile review correctly distinguishes this shear from the
   366	fold pullback to a two-component curve; the fold lemma alone does not
   367	transfer a kill.
   368	
   369	## 7. Exact residual and status
   370	
   371	The unresolved class can be stated without analogy.  With the twisted
   372	action `I -> det(g)^(-1) I((S,T)g)`, it is
   373	
   374	```text
   375	R_F^nm = { [I]_* : disc(I)=-27 kappa F,
   376	           B_I is a normal domain with generic group S_3,
   377	           B_I has the charged smooth/node local monodromy,
   378	           I(R^2) intersect C^* is empty } .           (7.1)
   379	```
   380	
   381	Since every complex unit has a cube root, the last condition is equivalent
   382	to the affine equation `I(S,T)=1` having no `R`-point.  The actual resolvent
   383	belongs to either the empty monogenic side, which section 6 kills, or to
   384	(7.1).  No source or argument here proves that the row's three nodes and
   385	one infinity place make (7.1) empty.
   386	
   387	| assertion | verdict |
   388	|---|---|
   389	| connected finite-flat normal `S_3` resolvent, reduced branch `D` | **PROVED**, consumed from charged r2 at its stated scope |
   390	| freeness implies a global power basis | **REFUTED** |
   391	| global power basis iff the binary cubic has a linear factor | **REFUTED**; the criterion is unit representation |
   392	| direct four-coefficient infinity/node bypass | **OPEN** |
   393	| generic-line consequence “low degree or extremal” | **REFUTED as stated**; it first leaves four bands |
   394	| row-specific exclusion of every high-cancellation band | **PROVED** by (5.1)--(5.5) |
   395	| no monogenic resolvent can carry the `(6,4)` row | **PROVED** |
   396	| unconditional uniform kill of `(6,4)` | **OPEN exactly at (7.1)** |
   397	
   398	The charged row geometry and its `(8,4)` routing remain provisional exactly
   399	as in the coordinator input.  No exit-price assertion is made.
