   205	## 4. What Davenport--Mason actually implies
   206	
   207	Assume now, and only in this section onward, that a global power basis has
   208	been supplied.  Rescale by nonzero complex constants and write its exact
   209	branch identity as
   210	
   211	```text
   212	A^3-B^2=F,       deg F=6,                              (4.1)
   213	```
   214	
   215	where `F` is the irreducible row equation.  If the leading terms do not
   216	cancel, (4.1) immediately gives `deg A<=2`, `deg B<=3`.  If either bound
   217	fails, equality of the two top degrees is necessary:
   218	
   219	```text
   220	deg A=2k,  deg B=3k,  k>=2.                            (4.2)
   221	```
   222	
   223	For coprime one-variable polynomials `P,Q,H` with `P^3-Q^2=H`, the
   224	Mason--Stothers theorem gives
   225	
   226	```text
   227	6k <= deg rad(PQH)-1 <= 2k+3k+deg H-1.                (4.3)
   228	```
   229	
   230	Equivalently Davenport's inequality is
   231	`deg(P^3-Q^2)>=k+1`.  With `deg H=6`, (4.3) yields only `k<=5`.
   232	Therefore the exact numerical alternatives before using row geometry are
   233	
   234	```text
   235	(deg A,deg B)=(4,6),(6,9),(8,12),(10,15).             (4.4)
   236	```
   237	
   238	This corrects the dichotomy in the charge.  Only `(10,15)` is a
   239	Davenport-extremal (DS order-five) case, since only there `deg H=k+1`.
   240	The first three pairs are the remaining nonextremal numerical bands and are
   241	not classified away by the Stothers--Zannier equality families.  Shioda's
   242	displayed order-five identity proves sharpness: its degree-six remainder is
   243	squarefree, although its zero set is a union of parallel lines and hence is
   244	not the row.
   245	
   246	For completeness, (4.3) is the direct polynomial-abc calculation applied
   247	to `P^3+(-Q^2)+(-H)=0`: pairwise coprimality makes the radical degree at
   248	most `2k+3k+6`, while the largest summand has degree `6k`.  Thus no
   249	classification assertion is hidden in the inequality.  Equality at `k=5`
   250	forces all three radicals to have their maximal degrees and gives the
   251	three-critical-value/Belyi description used by Stothers and Zannier, but a
   252	normalization of such a one-variable triple is allowed to vary with the
   253	chosen line.  Zannier's Proposition 2 encodes the equality cases by weighted
   254	trees (its order-five count is `D_0=4`), rather than by a single
   255	“Chebyshev-like” family.  Promoting any of those linewise normalizations to a
   256	fixed two-variable family would be invalid.
   257	
   258	## 5. Generic lines and the row-specific cancellation lemma
   259	
   260	The specialization used in section 4 is uniform on a nonempty Zariski-open
   261	set of affine lines.  The coefficient of the top power of the line parameter
   262	is the leading homogeneous form evaluated at its direction, so avoiding the
   263	three corresponding zero sets preserves the degrees of `A,B,F`.  A further
   264	open condition makes the line transverse to the reduced sextic and avoids
   265	its three nodes, hence `F|_ell` has six simple roots.
   266	
   267	There is no hidden gcd failure.  If `A(p)=B(p)=0`, then `F(p)=0` and `p` is
   268	singular.  The quadratic tangent cone of `A^3-B^2` is the square of the
   269	linear part of `B` when that part is nonzero; if it vanishes, the
   270	multiplicity is at least three.  Such a point is never an ordinary node.
   271	The row has no other affine singularities, so `V(A,B)` is empty.  Therefore
   272	the restrictions, and each of them with `F|_ell`, are pairwise coprime.
   273	This proves all hypotheses of (4.3) on one common open set and makes the
   274	degree-semicontinuity step airtight.
   275	
   276	The row geometry now eliminates all four bands in (4.4).  Let
   277	`C=V(F)`.  The images of `A,B` in `O(C)` have no zero, hence are units.  On
   278	`C`, `B^2=A^3`, so
   279	
   280	```text
   281	t=B/A,       t^2=A,       t^3=B.                      (5.1)
   282	```
   283	
   284	The projective normalization of `C` has exactly one point over infinity.
   285	The divisor of the pulled-back unit `t` is supported at that one point and
   286	has degree zero, so it is zero; a nowhere-zero regular function on the
   287	projective normalization is constant.  Thus `t=c in C^*`, and for some
   288	`P,Q in R`,
   289	
   290	```text
   291	A=c^2+FP,          B=c^3+FQ.                          (5.2)
   292	```
   293	
   294	In particular `F` divides the Jacobian `J(A,B)`.
   295	
   296	Suppose (4.2) holds.  Introduce the polynomial one-form
   297	
   298	```text
   299	omega=2A dB-3B dA.                                   (5.3)
   300	```
   301	
   302	Differentiating (4.1) and using `B^2=A^3-F` gives two exact identities:
   303	
   304	```text
   305	B omega=3F dA-A dF,        d omega=5 dA wedge dB.     (5.4)
   306	```
   307	
   308	The coefficients on the right of the first identity have degree at most
   309	`2k+5`.  Hence, if `omega` is nonzero, its coefficients have degree at most
   310	`5-k`.  For `k<=4` the second identity gives
   311	`deg J(A,B)<=4-k<6`; for `k=5` the coefficients of `omega` are constant, so
   312	the Jacobian is zero.  If `omega=0`, it is zero directly.  Since the
   313	irreducible degree-six polynomial `F` divides this Jacobian, in every case
   314	
   315	```text
   316	J(A,B)=0.                                              (5.5)
   317	```
   318	
   319	In characteristic zero, (5.5) implies that `A,B` are algebraically dependent
   320	by the criterion with Kahler differentials.  Use the polynomial Luroth
   321	lemma: two algebraically dependent polynomials in `C[x,y]` lie in `C[r]` for
   322	a polynomial `r`.  Here is the needed short
   323	justification.  Normalize `C[A,B]` in its fraction field.  A general line
   324	in `P^2` maps nontrivially to the smooth projective model of that field, so
   325	Riemann--Hurwitz makes the model `P^1`.  The normalization is therefore the
   326	coordinate ring of `P^1-S`.  It lies in the integrally closed ring
   327	`C[x,y]`: every element integral over `C[A,B]` is also integral over
   328	`C[x,y]` and belongs to its fraction field.  Hence all units of the
   329	normalization are constants.  The set `S` is nonempty because this is a
   330	positive-dimensional affine curve (if `S` were empty its ring would be
   331	`C`).  If `S` contained two points,
   332	`P^1-S` would have a nonconstant unit with divisor supported on those two
   333	points.  Hence `S` is one point and the normalization is `C[r]`, with
   334	`r in C[x,y]`.  Thus
   335	`A=U(r), B=V(r)` and `F=W(r)=U(r)^3-V(r)^2`.  Because `C` is algebraically
   336	closed and `F` is irreducible, `W` must be linear; otherwise its linear
   337	factors give a nontrivial factorization of `W(r)`.  Hence `r` is affine in
   338	`F` and `A,B` belong to `C[F]`.  Every positive degree among them is
   339	therefore divisible by six.  None of the four pairs in (4.4) has both
   340	degrees divisible by six.  This contradiction proves the promised exact
   341	lemma:
   342	
   343	```text
   344	If the depressed cubic has the row's irreducible one-place sextic branch
   345	and only ordinary affine nodes, then deg A<=2 and deg B<=3.              (5.6)
   346	```
   347	
   348	In particular the set of Davenport-extremal families compatible with the
   349	row is empty.  The argument treats the two-variable pair globally; it does
   350	not identify line-dependent normalizations.
   351	
